{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2020",
    "useDefineForClassFields": true,
    "module": "ESNext",
    /*
      No DOM on purpose. The transport seam is `@apollo/utils.fetcher`'s `Fetcher`, whose init allows
      `body?: Buffer`; a DOM `BodyInit` will not take a `Buffer<ArrayBufferLike>`, so with DOM in `lib` the
      ambient `fetch` stops being assignable and the providers lose their default. Node's declarations
      (undici-types) supply `fetch`, `Response`, `URL` and `AbortSignal` instead — the same flavour
      `tencent-doc-sdk` compiles against. A caller whose own project has DOM wraps its native fetch once.
    */
    "lib": ["ES2020"],
    "types": ["node"],
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    /* `@/…` is a module under `src/`, `@test/…` one under `test/`; the aliases are declared for vite in
       vite.config.ts, which is what vitest and the bundle resolve through, and `tsconfig.build.json` narrows
       the same mapping to `src/` alone because that is the public surface. Declarations are generated in the
       bundling pass by `unplugin-dts`, which resolves the aliases itself — a plain tsc emit would copy
       `@/…` verbatim into a file no consumer can read. */
    "paths": {
      "@/*": ["./src/*"],
      "@test/*": ["./test/*"]
    },
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "moduleDetection": "force",
    "noEmit": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "strict": true,
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "noUncheckedSideEffectImports": true
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts", "src/**/*.json", "test/**/*.ts", "vite.config.ts"]
}
